{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DAfKq3N2xXf7v24pzK5kPY","type":"tool_result","content":"\t\tt.Fatalf(\"OpenCloseouts = %+v, %v\", got, err)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestSetBranchAndPR(t *testing.T) {\n\tctx := tctx(t)\n\ts := newStore(t)\n\tj, err := s.CreateJob(ctx, NewJob{Stage: \"worker\", Role: \"eng\", Issue: 7})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif err := s.SetBranch(ctx, j.ID, \"worker/eng-7-x\"); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif err := s.SetPR(ctx, j.ID, 12); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tgot, _ := s.Job(ctx, j.ID)\n\tif got.Branch != \"worker/eng-7-x\" || got.PR != 12 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"job = %+v\", got)\n\t}\n\tfor name, err := range map[string]error{\n\t\t\"empty branch\": s.SetBranch(ctx, j.ID, \"\"),\n\t\t\"zero pr\":      s.SetPR(ctx, j.ID, 0),\n\t} {\n\t\tif err == nil {\nstore_test.go:21:func newStore(t *testing.T) *Store {\nstore_test.go:27:func newStoreClock(t *testing.T, dir string) (*Store, *clock) {\n72:    files from the network, answer needs-human and list the inputs you\n76:    already exists, answer needs-human instead.\n89:of these holds the change for a human, and nothing is pushed:\n127:   - needs-human when:\n133:     - there is nothing to change, because the default branch already does\n149:   If a dependency change would exceed them, answer needs-human.\n154:   fixed within it, change nothing more, keep status done and explain\n159:   untracked file must belong to the change, and nothing under \"What the\n161:8. Write .ard/N/worker-engineer.md last. On blocked or needs-human, leave\n172:  - status: done, needs-human or blocked\n120:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n278:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request), `designer` (`## UX`, plus Markdown or script-free SVG files under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`) and `documentation-planner` (`## Docs`: which docs change and what each must say). The command line counts as a user interface, so the designer covers flags, output and messages. The starter ordering is product-manager, then architect; designer, then documentation-planner; the engineer runs after architect and documentation-planner (`after:` in the starter `config.yaml`). Note for #45: acceptance criteria are a numbered list under `### Acceptance criteria`. The starter prompts read the repo context docs per role: `product-manager` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`, `architect` reads `VISION.md` and `TECH_STACK.md`, `designer` and `documentation-planner` read `PRODUCT.md`, and `engineer` reads `TECH_STACK.md`. Each prompt states the shared rules: the file is optional, one with no text outside headings and HTML comments counts as absent, the issue is the ask and the files are constraints, and `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` win on commands and layout. A planning role that finds a conflict records it, with file and section, in its own role file.\n360:4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and, in a fix step, `.ard-input/<issue#>/test-output.log` (the untrusted header, the test command and its exit code, and the last 64 KiB of the test log). In a review round, `.ard-input/<issue#>/review-feedback.md` (the untrusted header, the round, the committed role files of the reviewers that asked for changes and the pull request comments of people, cut at 64 KiB), and the reviewer role files and logs on the branch are untrusted inputs too. The agent edits code and writes `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`; the executor scrubs a stale copy first.\n431:**Branch protection and the bot.** The committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to `main` with `[skip ci]`, which neither a \"require a pull request\" rule nor a required status check can admit, so the App must be on the bypass list of `main`'s ruleset (mode Always). GitHub's bypass covers the whole ruleset, not single rules (found on Gate 2, #39). ard keeps that bypass narrow itself: the committer refuses any path outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, the merger merges only when every review passed and required checks are green on the current head (checked in code, not left to GitHub), and the bot-commit audit flags any bot commit on `main` that touches anything else. GitHub enforces nothing on the App's merges, so the merger reads the required checks itself from the branch rules API. A squash merge has GitHub (`noreply@github.com`) as committer and the App as author, so the audit, which keys on the committer, does not cover merges: the merger's gates do.\n517:**`ard setup` applies `.ard/` to GitHub (decided, #303).** It reads the local `./.ard/` and warns when it differs from `origin/main`, which `ard serve` runs. With no flags it runs every check, then applies only if all of them are green; `--check` reports only (exit 2 on drift) and `--dry-run` routes writes through the effect layer. Each row is `ok`, `changed`, `drift` or `manual` with the exact fix. It **writes** only two things: the `ard:*` label catalogue (create and update, never delete; `ard labels sync` stays as an alias) and the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`, derived from the App's own identity (`<slug>[bot]`), never from config. Variables need the operator's token (`gh auth token` or a PAT, #302), read once, held in memory and never logged, stored or passed to an agent or job. It **only checks**, against a policy built into ard rather than repo content, and prints the fix on drift: the shared permission check; `main`'s active ruleset requires a pull request and its bypass list is exactly the ard App in mode Always; the App has no `workflows` or `administration` permission; merge settings (`allow_rebase_merge` off once versioning is on); and `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` on `origin/main` against a copy embedded in ard (`ard setup --print-workflow` prints it for a human to commit). Setup never commits to the repository, `.github/` included, and never writes rulesets, bypass actors or App settings: a merged config change must not be able to loosen branch protection through an admin token. `ard setup --create-gh-app` (#350) creates the App itself with GitHub's manifest flow.\n525:- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read). The merger reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branch rules (metadata). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\n572:- **Checks.** The Jenkins multibranch job `ard-ci` builds every same-repository branch and pull request, `worker/*` branches included, but never forks, with `make lint`, `make test`, `make build` and `make chart-test`. Its pipeline is loaded from the deployment repository, never from the branch, so branch code supplies only those targets and runs in a pod with no credential and no service account token. `main` requires both its status, `continuous-integration/jenkins/pr-merge`, and the Actions `test` job ([#219](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/219)): Jenkins stops reporting to a PR it has seen close, so a PR that merged before Jenkins finished kept a `pending` status for good. A fork PR gets no Jenkins build, so a maintainer pushes it to a same-repository branch to merge it. `ci.yml` and `bot-commits.yml` stay as they are.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0f53e436-51a7-4416-9cdc-1117f8238899","uuid":"493c32d1-acc0-4e42-93d4-83626c75a864","timestamp":"2026-11-25T16:59:05.925Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\t\tt.Fatalf(\"OpenCloseouts = %+v, %v\", got, err)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestSetBranchAndPR(t *testing.T) {\n\tctx := tctx(t)\n\ts := newStore(t)\n\tj, err := s.CreateJob(ctx, NewJob{Stage: \"worker\", Role: \"eng\", Issue: 7})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif err := s.SetBranch(ctx, j.ID, \"worker/eng-7-x\"); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif err := s.SetPR(ctx, j.ID, 12); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tgot, _ := s.Job(ctx, j.ID)\n\tif got.Branch != \"worker/eng-7-x\" || got.PR != 12 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"job = %+v\", got)\n\t}\n\tfor name, err := range map[string]error{\n\t\t\"empty branch\": s.SetBranch(ctx, j.ID, \"\"),\n\t\t\"zero pr\":      s.SetPR(ctx, j.ID, 0),\n\t} {\n\t\tif err == nil {\nstore_test.go:21:func newStore(t *testing.T) *Store {\nstore_test.go:27:func newStoreClock(t *testing.T, dir string) (*Store, *clock) {\n72:    files from the network, answer needs-human and list the inputs you\n76:    already exists, answer needs-human instead.\n89:of these holds the change for a human, and nothing is pushed:\n127:   - needs-human when:\n133:     - there is nothing to change, because the default branch already does\n149:   If a dependency change would exceed them, answer needs-human.\n154:   fixed within it, change nothing more, keep status done and explain\n159:   untracked file must belong to the change, and nothing under \"What the\n161:8. Write .ard/N/worker-engineer.md last. On blocked or needs-human, leave\n172:  - status: done, needs-human or blocked\n120:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, an